A LIST ITEM HOLDING ONLY A LINK REFERENCE DEFINITION LOSES THE DEFINITION.

WHAT md DOES TODAY. The bullet survives and the URL does not:

    # Title

    -

WHAT IT SHOULD DO. Write the definition back inside its item. The URL appears
nowhere else in the file, and `format` writes the file, so one run ends it.

THIS IS NOT A cmark BUG, AND `[ref]: /url` IS NOT A DROPPED LINK.
It is a link reference DEFINITION, a block level construct that by design
renders nothing: it defines a label for later `[ref]` links to point at. cmark
stores it in the document's reference map rather than leaving a node in the
tree, which is what the spec asks for. Proof, if it is ever doubted again:

    "[ref]: /url\n\nSee [ref] here.\n"   ->   "See [ref](/url) here.\n"

The definition RESOLVED. md then pastes the URL inline, because that is all it
has left. Brackets alone are not a link and never were: `[ref] /url` comes
back as `\[ref] /url`, escaped as the ordinary text it is. The colon is what
makes a definition, and `(...)` is what makes an inline link.

WHAT CHANGED HERE, AND A CLAIM CORRECTED.
This case first recorded that the whole LIST vanished — `md blocks` said
`unordered list(0 items)` — so a block the author wrote disappeared entire.
Fixing `format-keeps-an-empty-list-item` recovered the bullet, because an item
whose definition cmark ate looks exactly like an item written empty: both have
no children.

That earlier marker said the empty-item fix would invent "a bullet nobody
wrote". That was wrong and is withdrawn. The author DID write this bullet; the
`- ` is in the source. Only the definition is lost, which is a smaller defect
than the one it replaced, and strictly better than losing the block. The
remaining loss is real but it is of one kind, not two.

CAUSE AND FIX, owned jointly with `format-keeps-an-unused-link-reference-definition`.
That case tracks the same loss at the top level of a document and states the
cause: `MarkdownParser.parse` never sees a definition, so nothing can write one
back. Keeping it needs the definitions read out of the SOURCE before the tree
is walked, since the parser will not hand them back. Inside a list item the fix
needs one thing more — knowing which item the definition belongs to, so it goes
back inside its bullet rather than after the list.
